    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. we've had some issues with batches in the center staying warm for too long. what are the best practices for the actual cooling methods (like ice baths vs blast chillers) to make sure we don't get any spots where the bacteria could take hold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Blast chillers are generally superior to ice baths for poultry prep because they provide rapid, uniform airflow that prevents the \"thermal inheritance\" where hot cores prevent the exterior from cooling fast enough. When using ice baths, you must continuously mix or recirculate the bath water to avoid stagnant pockets that create warm spots conducive to bacterial growth. To ensure no bacterial holdouts, your cooling stations should have a maximum thermal mass (like a large batch container) so that the cooling surface area-to-volume ratio remains high, allowing the entire batch to drop below 70°F quickly and consistently.",
